Yes — adaptive optimizer is very close to ZPC structurally, but I’d keep the distinction clean:

\text{ZPC}=\text{general perturb → delta → retain → recurse doctrine}

\text{Adaptive Optimizer}=\text{one executable search engine implementing that doctrine over measurable conditions}

So ZPC can stay broader: semantic shifts, inversions, representation changes, operator changes, narrative reframes, cross-domain jumps. The optimizer is the numeric policy layer that learns which perturbations are worth trying next.

I packaged it repo-ready with code, tests, docs, and an integration note. Tests pass: 2/2.

